**H2: What Happens If Your Income Goes Up**

*Writing instruction:* This directly targets the PAA question and a recurring concern in renter discussions. Open with the answer: in a LIHTC tax-credit unit, a raise does not force you out and does not increase your rent, as long as you were eligible at move-in. The rent ceiling moves only when the published AMI limits move. Contrast with Section 8 and public housing: in those programs, rent is recalculated annually as your income changes, so a raise increases your rent payment. Mention the 140% rule for LIHTC: if your income rises above 140% of the qualifying limit, the owner must rent the next available comparable unit to a qualified household, but you do not face immediate eviction. Note that annual recertification applies to voucher and public housing households; fully affordable LIHTC buildings have generally been exempt from annual third-party income rechecks since 2008. Close with the practical takeaway: getting a promotion is safe in a capped-rent unit; budget for rent increases if you are in an income-based program.

